[39m←  ☒ Commits [38;5;16m[48;5;153m ☐ truncate(n) [39m[49m ✔ Submit  →

[1m[38;5;231mFor truncate(text, n), how should n and the ellipsis interact?[0m

[38;5;153m❯[39m [38;5;246m1.[39m [38;5;153mn caps the total, with "..." (Recommended)[39m
     [38;5;246mResult is never longer than n characters; the 3-char ASCII "..." is included in that budget. truncate('abcdefg', 5)
[39m     [38;5;246m=== 'ab...'. Predictable for fixed-width layouts.[39m
  [38;5;246m2.[39m n caps the total, with "…"
     [38;5;246mSame budget rule but a single-character Unicode ellipsis, so more text survives: truncate('abcdefg', 5) === [39m
     [38;5;246m'abcd…'.[39m
  [38;5;246m3.[39m n counts text only
     [38;5;246mCut to n characters of source text, then append the marker, so output can be n+3 characters. truncate('abcdefg', 5)
[39m     [38;5;246m=== 'abcde...'.[39m
  [38;5;246m4.[39m Truncate on word boundary
     [38;5;246mNever cut mid-word: back off to the last whitespace before the limit, then append the marker. Nicer prose, slightly
[39m     [38;5;246mmore edge cases to test.[39m
